{"results": [{"id": "hp_doc_6ccb0412687ca268ef1ab4c549073e16cfa58efae5b351c1b0e26bf994912266", "title": "The Gambia", "text": "The Gambia ( ), officially the Republic of The Gambia, is a country in West Africa that is entirely surrounded by Senegal except for its coastline on the Atlantic Ocean at its western end. It is the smallest country in mainland Africa.", "score": 30.6298}, {"id": "hp_doc_6e53f40c32e20f0b3f70b8f34394f7d1f74d85635a26b47ece047f60622b0ba5", "title": "Niumi National Park", "text": "Niumi National Park is a national park in The Gambia. The occupies the coastal strip in the northern region of the country, in the southern tip of the Sine-Saloum Delta. It covers an area of approximately 4,940 ha (49.4 square km) and encompasses a range of types of wetlands and vegetation, from freshwater marsh to sand spits and brackish lagoons. \" Rhizophora\" mangrove forest is abundant in the park, and its swamp and mudflats are an important sheltering ground for birds, with over 200 species found here.", "score": 6.848}, {"id": "hp_doc_28225291323453c04c9a8c8386f6792b4112fdebe1bcc128df6f3a4bcbfbaa0e", "title": "West African Airways Corporation", "text": "West African Airways Corporation, or WAAC for short, was an airline that operated from 1946 to 1958, jointly owned by the governments of Britain's four west African colonies, namely The Gambia, the Gold Coast (now Ghana), Nigeria, and Sierra Leone. The carrier was headquartered at the \"Airways House\" in Ikeja, Nigeria, and operated from its hub in Lagos Airport. It was dissolved on none }} , after all the shareholder countries but Nigeria set up their own national airlines following their independence. As the sole remaining major stockholder of the airline, the government of Nigeria continued to operate it as WAAC Nigeria, which was eventually renamed Nigeria Airways and became the flag carrier of the country.", "score": 6.0881}], "remaining_searches": 1}
